{"kind":"assert","text":"Hugh: extend the adapter-compatibility check to request writing, not only reading.\n\nThe [earlier compatibility review](notes/2026-09-05-resident-adapter-coherence.md@32660749aad93bff40c93f6aedf6b80a186dd489:15) showed an older loaded adapter failing to decode a newer resident response. Today the failure was quieter: my configured direct MCP writer accepted target_ref and no_git_artifact in request bodies but emitted workroom/state@2. The current [request preparation boundary](internal/app/app.go@32660749aad93bff40c93f6aedf6b80a186dd489:1474) emits state@3 and captures the landing result. The fold's [documented legacy behavior](docs/reference/gs/merge.md@32660749aad93bff40c93f6aedf6b80a186dd489:205) is deliberately different. Successful append was therefore insufficient evidence that the requested result was governed as intended.\n\nI checked the signed Intent schemas of npm69e31619, S2dc7f7d11, clarification6eee19d1 and record-guide12b0b1ae; they were state@2. Codex's contemporaneous review requeste4e2ed01 was state@3. Native inspection marked the completed npm assignment legacy. Its approved source still landed to the intended main and passed actual CI/Security, so I did not reopen that delivery. I replaced the four affected open assignments, preserving their full outcomes, after switching my own bridge to an adapter built at32660749. The replacements have verified state@3 Intents: three carry the explicit main target triple; the clarification carries only no_git_artifact=true. Correction31327bff records the breakdown. No unintended source merge or authority bypass is alleged. Non-request state@2 records are normal and are not evidence of this defect.\n\nRecommendation: add one writing check to the existing deployment/connection check. Before a session writes requests using a newly governed result contract, establish that its actual loaded producer supports that contract. The check must exercise or inspect the prepared request through that connection before append, including both target_ref and no_git_artifact shapes; a fresh CLI or a current repository checkout does not prove what another loaded adapter emits. Report a clear reconnect/upgrade action when the needed contract is unavailable. Use semantic compatibility, not equality with the latest source hash, and create no disposable durable request merely to test connectivity.\n\nKeep the change small: pair the resident's accepted request contract with the adapter's produced contract in the existing compatibility design; add focused controls for an old writer, a compatible writer and a renewed connection, proving the produced Intent and result fields rather than just a successful response. Preserve deliberately supported historical reads and legacy acts, strict decoding, actor identity, signatures, liveness and port ownership. Do not turn a compatibility mismatch into permission to reset a witness, restart someone else's host, or weaken admission.\n\nThis extends the earlier recommendation instead of starting a competing deployment plan. It is a review note, not an adopted protocol change or an implementation assignment. The observed cost is four open assignments replaced and their evidence rechecked; I make no estimate of general time savings. A stronger write check would prevent that avoidable recovery work.","body":{"head":"32660749aad93bff40c93f6aedf6b80a186dd489"}}